      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's dive into the chemistry.\n\nThe Maillard reaction involves the reaction between amino acids (R-NH2) and reducing sugars (R-CHO), resulting in the formation of new compounds with distinct flavors, aromas, and colors. This reaction is often represented by the following general equation:\n\nR-NH2 + R-CHO → R-NH-CO-R' + H2O\n\nWhere R and R' are the resulting compounds.\n\nIn contrast, caramelization involves the reaction between reducing sugars (R-CHO) and heat, resulting in the formation of new compounds with a characteristic sweet, caramel flavor. This reaction is often represented by the following general equation:\n\nR-CHO → R-CO-R' + H2O\n\nNotice the difference in the reactants and products. The Maillard reaction involves both amino acids and reducing sugars, while caramelization involves only reducing sugars.\n\nThink of it like a game of molecular tag, where the goblins (molecules) are chasing each other around the kitchen, forming new bonds and compounds as they go. The Maillard reaction is like a more complex, multi-player game, while caramelization is like a simpler, one-on",
